WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.280
We'll spend a few minutes going over load order in the clod.md hierarchy because this is really

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.199
important. What happens when instructions at different levels overlap? How clod code concatenates

3
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:17.680
them? Why a genuine conflict has no reliable winner? How to write configs that minimize

4
00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:23.520
conflict in the first place? And the anti-patterns that quietly break team consistency without any

5
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:31.280
error message? Let's get into it. Picture a clod.md in your home directory and another one in the repo.

6
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.560
Both open in the same session and they don't quite agree. What happens next is what this

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:44.240
lesson is about. Load order is the place to start. Clod code concatenates your clod.md files

8
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.840
broadest to most specific. When two instructions genuinely conflict, there is no reliable

9
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:56.000
precedence rule. Clod may follow either one, so don't count on the closer file winning. That's why

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.520
avoiding contradictions matters. Complementary instructions are the far more common case.

11
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.319
Instructions at different levels that simply don't conflict at all. When instructions are

12
00:01:06.319 --> 00:01:12.639
complementary, both apply simultaneously without any resolution step needed. The model follows

13
00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:18.800
both. The config anti-pattern is placing team standards in user-level config rather than

14
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:25.279
project-level. Other contributors don't see user-level config, so placing team rules there

15
00:01:25.279 --> 00:01:32.000
breaks consistency silently with no warning or error. Here's how overlap actually works.

16
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:38.559
Clod code doesn't pick one file and throw the others away. It concatenates all your clod.md

17
00:01:38.559 --> 00:01:45.839
files into one combined set of instructions, read broadest to most specific. So most of the time,

18
00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:51.919
user-level and project-level guidance simply stacks and both apply. When two instructions

19
00:01:51.919 --> 00:01:58.080
genuinely conflict on the same point, there is no reliable precedence. Clod may follow either one.

20
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.760
Think of a project rule about formatting sitting alongside a contradictory personal style note.

21
00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.399
You can't count on the project rule shaping the result. There's no rule engine that

22
00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:14.320
deterministically declares a winner. The practical takeaway is to avoid writing directly contradictory

23
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.759
instructions across levels. Design them to complement each other, and you never have to guess

24
00:02:19.759 --> 00:02:25.759
which one won. It's worth being clear about why you can't just lean on the project file to win.

25
00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:31.600
Project-level config represents the team's agreed standards. It's version-controlled,

26
00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:37.520
committed to the repo, and visible to every contributor. But when a personal instruction

27
00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.639
genuinely contradicts it, there's no rule that reliably resolves toward the project file.

28
00:02:43.199 --> 00:02:49.520
User-level config, by contrast, is invisible to teammates. It lives on one person's machine and

29
00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.639
applies only to their sessions. That's exactly the right home for individual preference.

30
00:02:54.639 --> 00:03:01.039
The thing to internalize is that concatenation is additive by nature. Both files stay in context.

31
00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:06.479
So the way to keep shared standards reliable isn't to lean on a hard override rule.

32
00:03:06.479 --> 00:03:11.039
It's to keep personal and team instructions from contradicting each other in the first place.

33
00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.679
Then there's never a question about which one applied. Resolution only matters when instructions

34
00:03:17.679 --> 00:03:22.320
actually overlap. An overlap means two instructions address the same behavior

35
00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:29.600
with opposing directions. User says tabs. Project says spaces. Their clod may pick either one.

36
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:34.320
So it's cleaner to just not write the conflict. When instructions are complementary

37
00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:39.919
and address different behaviors, both simply apply. User says be concise in responses.

38
00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:45.039
Project says always add type hints. These two address completely different things.

39
00:03:45.039 --> 00:03:51.199
There's no overlap, so both apply. Clod code is concise and adds type hints. Understanding

40
00:03:51.199 --> 00:03:57.679
the complementary case is important because most clod.md combinations don't overlap at all.

41
00:03:57.679 --> 00:04:02.479
Concatenation is additive by default. Overlap resolution is the exception,

42
00:04:02.479 --> 00:04:07.679
not the standard operating mode. The best way to avoid conflicts is to be

43
00:04:07.679 --> 00:04:13.759
intentional about scope at the design stage. User-level config should only contain instructions

44
00:04:13.759 --> 00:04:19.920
that genuinely don't belong to any specific project. Personal tone preferences, individual

45
00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:26.799
workflow shortcuts, output format defaults. These are good user-level content. Avoid putting

46
00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:33.440
formatting rules or coding conventions at user level if any project you work on has a project

47
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:41.359
level clod.md that covers the same topic. At the project level, write all project-specific coding

48
00:04:41.359 --> 00:04:48.160
standards explicitly, even for topics your user-level config also addresses. Since a genuine

49
00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.720
conflict resolves unpredictably, making the intended rule explicit avoids confusion about

50
00:04:54.720 --> 00:05:00.320
which instruction is in effect and makes the project config self-contained for new contributors.

51
00:05:01.279 --> 00:05:07.359
The most dangerous config mistake is putting team standards in user-level config. It's dangerous

52
00:05:07.359 --> 00:05:13.200
because it fails silently. There's no error. Clod code doesn't warn you that a team standard wasn't

53
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.959
loaded for your colleagues. Your sessions look fine because your user-level config is loaded,

54
00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:24.720
but every other contributor's sessions run without those instructions. The team loses consistency in

55
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:29.600
a way that's hard to diagnose. A developer wondering why their pair programming partner is

56
00:05:29.839 --> 00:05:34.720
getting different clod code behavior won't immediately think to check each other's user-level

57
00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:40.320
configs. The fix is simple. Any instruction that should apply to the whole team belongs in the

58
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:47.600
project level clod.md committed to source control. If you want to see which instructions clod code

59
00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:53.359
is actually following in a session, you have a few practical options. The most direct is the

60
00:05:53.359 --> 00:05:59.359
slash memory command, which lists the loaded memory files, and the slash context command,

61
00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:04.399
which lets you inspect what's currently loaded into context. There's no automatically printed

62
00:06:04.399 --> 00:06:09.920
list of files at startup, so don't go looking for one. If you want that logged, an instructions

63
00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:15.920
loaded hook can capture it, and you can test overlapping instructions explicitly. Set a user

64
00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:22.000
scope instruction, set an opposing project scope instruction, and see which guidance actually takes

65
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:27.760
effect. This kind of verification is particularly useful when you're onboarding to a new project

66
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:34.000
and want to confirm the project's clod.md is loaded and active, or when you're debugging

67
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:40.559
inconsistent behavior between your sessions and a teammate's sessions. Let's make this concrete

68
00:06:40.559 --> 00:06:47.839
with a real example. A team adds a no semicolons rule to their project level clod.md developer.

69
00:06:48.399 --> 00:06:55.359
A has always use semicolons in their user level config. Developer B has no semicolon rule in

70
00:06:55.359 --> 00:07:01.839
their user level config at all. Both developers open clod code on this project. For developer B,

71
00:07:01.839 --> 00:07:06.880
there's no conflict, so the project rule just applies, and there are no semicolons.

72
00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:12.640
Developer A is the interesting case, because now two instructions directly contradict.

73
00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.640
And here's the honest answer. That outcome is non-deterministic.

74
00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.320
Clod may follow the project rule, or it may follow the user rule. You can't count on the

75
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.160
more specific file winning. The right move is to avoid the conflict entirely, and if two scopes

76
00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:34.000
might overlap, state the precedence you want explicitly in the more specific file, so the

77
00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:41.359
intended rule is unambiguous. People hear load order and assume precedence, but those aren't

78
00:07:41.359 --> 00:07:46.880
the same thing. Overlapping instructions stack because the files get concatenated rather than

79
00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:52.799
ranked. Contradictory ones have no reliable winner, and nothing warns you either way,

80
00:07:52.799 --> 00:07:59.200
so the job isn't picking which layer wins. It's keeping team standards visible in project files

81
00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.640
and writing rules that stack instead of collide with each other.

82
00:08:03.279 --> 00:08:06.480
That brings this one to a close. I'll see you in the next lesson.

